/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Critical thinking batch Y */
WB.L("philosophical-razors", "thinking", "beginner", "Philosophical razors: shaving away bad explanations",
  "Your car won't start. Is it a flat battery, or sabotage by a jealous neighbour? A handful of simple rules called 'razors' can save you from chasing the wrong answer.",
  [
    ["What a razor is", "A razor is a rule of thumb that shaves away unlikely explanations to show where to look first, without proving anything.",
      "Picture a detective with ten suspects and one afternoon. She needs a way to decide where to look first. Thinkers have long used short rules for exactly this job, nicknamed 'razors' because they shave away explanations that are possible but improbable.\n\nA razor never proves anything. It doesn't tell you the answer is definitely the simple one. It tells you where the smart money is, so you can spend your attention sensibly. Think of razors as tie breakers and starting points, not verdicts. Used well, they stop you getting lost in elaborate theories. Used badly, as a way to end every conversation, they become a lazy excuse not to think."],
    ["Occam's razor", "When two explanations fit the facts equally well, favour the one that needs fewer assumptions.",
      "The most famous razor is named after William of Ockham, an English friar and philosopher who lived around 1287 to 1347. The slogan 'entities should not be multiplied beyond necessity' was written by later scholars, but the idea runs through his work.\n\nIn plain terms: when two explanations fit the facts equally well, favour the one that asks you to assume less. If your biscuits vanish, a hungry teenager requires one assumption. A burglar who ignored your laptop and took only the Tim Tams requires several.\n\nNotice the key phrase: equally well. Occam's razor doesn't say simple explanations are always true. Complexity has to earn its place by explaining something the simpler version can't."],
    ["Hanlon's razor", "Don't assume bad intent when a mistake, busyness or ignorance would explain things, unless a pattern says otherwise.",
      "A colleague leaves you off an important email chain. You might think they are freezing you out. Hanlon's razor suggests a gentler first guess: they probably forgot.\n\nThe usual wording is 'never attribute to malice that which is adequately explained by stupidity', credited to a man named Robert Hanlon, who submitted it to a joke book about Murphy's Law around 1980. A kinder version: don't assume bad intent when busyness, ignorance or a simple mistake would do.\n\nIt helps because our brains are quick to see enemies, and it saves friendships and wasted anger. It isn't naive, though. If the 'mistakes' keep happening in one direction, the pattern itself becomes evidence."],
    ["Hitchens's razor and horses", "Claims need supporting evidence from the person making them, and common explanations should be ruled out before rare ones.",
      "Someone says a celebrity is secretly a lizard. You ask for evidence, and they reply, 'Prove they're not.' Christopher Hitchens's answer: 'What can be asserted without evidence can also be dismissed without evidence.' The person making a claim should supply reasons. You can set it aside until they do. That doesn't mean it is false.\n\nMedical students learn: when you hear hoofbeats, think of horses, not zebras. A headache and runny nose is most likely a cold, not an exotic disease. If your internet drops, a tired router is a horse and a targeted hack is a zebra. This is about base rates, how often each explanation occurs. Zebras do exist, so rule out the horses first."],
    ["When razors cut wrong", "Razors set your starting odds, not your conclusion, so follow the evidence when it points to the complicated answer.",
      "By the 1840s, astronomers knew Uranus wasn't moving quite as the maths predicted. The simple response would have been small measurement errors. Instead, some argued for a more complicated answer: an unseen planet tugging on it. In 1846, Neptune was found close to where the calculations said it would be. The elaborate explanation was right because it explained what the simpler one couldn't.\n\nThat is how to treat every razor. People are sometimes genuinely malicious, and zebras sometimes turn up. When something surprising happens, ask what simple explanation fits all the facts, could it be a mistake, and who is making the claim. Treat your first answer as a working hypothesis. A razor that stops you looking becomes a blindfold."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a philosophical razor best used for?", ["Proving which explanation is correct","Deciding which explanation to investigate first","Ending a debate by picking the simplest answer","Showing that complicated explanations are always wrong"], 1, "A razor shows where the smart money is, but it never proves an explanation."],
    [1, "According to Occam's razor, when should you favour the simpler explanation?", ["Whenever it is easier to understand","Whenever it was proposed first","When it fits the facts equally well","When it has more supporters"], 2, "Simplicity only wins when explanations fit equally well, since complexity must earn its place."],
    [2, "Why is Hanlon's razor useful?", ["It guards against assuming bad intent too fast","It proves other people are always well-meaning","It tells you to ignore repeated mistakes","It shows other people are usually careless"], 0, "Our brains are quick to see enemies, though a repeated one-sided pattern is still evidence worth noticing."],
    [3, "Under Hitchens's razor, who must supply the evidence for a claim?", ["The person doubting the claim","An independent expert chosen by both sides","Nobody, as claims can't be tested","The person making the claim"], 3, "What can be asserted without evidence can be dismissed without evidence, so the burden sits with the claimant."],
    [4, "What does the discovery of Neptune teach about razors?", ["The simplest explanation is usually right","Evidence can justify a complicated answer","Measurement errors explain most odd results","Unseen causes should never be considered"], 1, "The elaborate explanation was right because it explained something the simple one couldn't."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A simple rule can stop you chasing the wrong explanation, until it stops you thinking."});

WB.L("conspiracy-theories", "thinking", "beginner", "Why conspiracy theories feel so convincing",
  "Real conspiracies happen, yet most grand theories about secret plots fall apart. Learn why they grip clever people, and how to tell a real cover-up from a story.",
  [
    ["Some conspiracies are real", "Real conspiracies happen, but they are uncovered through documents, whistleblowers and court cases, not by theories that never produce solid evidence.",
      "In 1972, burglars were caught inside the Democratic Party's offices in the Watergate building in Washington. Over two years, journalists and investigators uncovered a cover-up that reached the White House, and President Richard Nixon resigned in 1974. For decades, tobacco companies also played down what their own scientists knew about smoking's harms.\n\nSo powerful people sometimes collude and lie. That is what makes conspiracy theories plausible. The question isn't whether conspiracies ever happen. It is how to tell real ones, uncovered through documents, whistleblowers and court cases, from the many theories that never produce solid evidence however long people look."],
    ["Why they feel true", "Conspiracy theories appeal because we expect big events to have big causes, over-detect patterns, and find comfort in a world someone controls.",
      "When something shocking happens, a small, random cause feels insulting. Psychologists call this proportionality bias: a big effect seems to need a big explanation. The 1963 assassination of John F. Kennedy is a classic case. A lone gunman changing history feels wrong, so a grand plot seems fitting. Recognise when an explanation appeals mainly because it feels emotionally right.\n\nOur ancestors who assumed a predator in a rustle in the grass were safer than those who assumed wind. Many researchers think this shaped minds that over-detect patterns and intentions. Conspiracy theories turn chaos into a story with villains and a plan. That can be comforting, since someone is in charge. People are more drawn to them when anxious, powerless or uncertain."],
    ["The self-sealing story", "A theory that no evidence could ever disprove tells you nothing about the world.",
      "Here is a warning sign worth memorising. Ask a believer what evidence would change their mind. If the answer is 'nothing', or every piece of contrary evidence is explained away as part of the plot, the theory has become self-sealing. Missing evidence proves the cover-up. Official denials prove the cover-up. Experts who disagree are in on it.\n\nThis structure feels powerful because it can't be refuted, but that is exactly its weakness. A claim that survives every possible observation tells you nothing about the world.\n\nReal investigations work the other way. Journalists who exposed Watergate could point to money trails, tapes and witnesses. When a theory's main evidence is the absence of evidence, be cautious."],
    ["The problem of numbers", "Large conspiracies are hard to keep secret because the more people involved, the faster someone talks.",
      "The Apollo programme employed hundreds of thousands of people across government agencies, universities and private companies. A hoax would have needed all of them, plus rival scientists in the Soviet Union who tracked the missions, to stay silent for over fifty years.\n\nIn 2016, physicist David Grimes published a model estimating how quickly large conspiracies would be likely to leak. His rough conclusion was that secrets involving thousands of people tend to come out within a few years.\n\nYou don't need the maths to see the logic. People talk, change jobs, write memoirs, fall out with their bosses and seek fame. Real conspiracies usually involve small groups, and even those are often exposed eventually."],
    ["Motives and talking it through", "Ask who benefits from you believing a story, and when talking with a believer, use curiosity and keep the relationship.",
      "Ask not only who benefits from the alleged plot but who benefits from you believing it. Some promoters sell supplements, courses or subscriptions. Others earn advertising money, and outrage keeps people watching. Some foreign governments have spread divisive stories to weaken trust in other countries' institutions. None of this proves a claim false, but apply scepticism evenly, to governments and corporations and to influencers telling you what they are hiding.\n\nIf someone you care about is deep in a rabbit hole, mockery backfires. Ask genuine questions: what first made you think this, and what would change your mind? Find common ground and share your own uncertainty. Prebunking, such as Cambridge's online games, teaches manipulation tricks in advance. People rarely leave a belief by losing an argument, but often return through trusted friends."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How are real conspiracies usually uncovered?", ["Through anonymous online rumours","Through repeated claims that they must exist","Through documents, whistleblowers and court cases","Through the absence of any official records"], 2, "Real cover-ups such as Watergate were exposed with specific evidence, unlike theories that never produce any."],
    [1, "What is proportionality bias?", ["The sense that a big event needs a big explanation","The tendency to trust small groups over large ones","The belief that most events are caused by chance","The habit of favouring official explanations"], 0, "A random or small cause can feel inadequate for a shocking event, which makes grand plots appealing."],
    [2, "Why is a self-sealing theory a warning sign?", ["It is always based on an outright lie","It is always spread by governments","It cannot be explained to other people","Nothing could ever count against it"], 3, "A claim that survives every possible observation can't be tested, so it tells you nothing about the world."],
    [3, "Why does the number of people involved matter for a conspiracy?", ["Larger groups are more careful with secrets","More people means more chances of a leak","Big projects are always too complex to hide","Only small groups can be investigated"], 1, "People talk, change jobs and write memoirs, so secrets held by thousands tend to come out within years."],
    [4, "What is a better way to talk with someone who believes a conspiracy theory?", ["Mock the claims until they back down","Ask genuine questions and keep the relationship","Win the argument with a long list of facts","Avoid the subject and cut contact"], 1, "Mockery confirms the story that outsiders are closed-minded, while trusted friends are how people often return."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some conspiracies are real. Here's how to tell which ones, and why the others stick."});

WB.L("cold-reading-barnum-effect", "thinking", "beginner", "Cold reading: how psychics seem to know you",
  "'You have a great deal of unused potential.' Sounds just like you, doesn't it? Discover the simple tricks that make strangers seem to read your mind.",
  [
    ["A horoscope for everyone", "We accept vague, general statements as uniquely true of ourselves, a tendency called the Forer or Barnum effect.",
      "In 1948, psychologist Bertram Forer gave his students a personality test, then handed each a supposedly personal analysis. On average they rated it above four out of five for accuracy. Then came the twist: every student had received exactly the same text, assembled largely from astrology material. It included lines such as 'you have a great need for other people to like and admire you' and 'at times you are extroverted, affable, sociable, while at other times you are introverted, wary, reserved'.\n\nThis tendency to accept general statements as uniquely true of ourselves is called the Forer effect, or the Barnum effect, after the showman P. T. Barnum. Later studies have repeated it many times."],
    ["Barnum statements", "Barnum statements sound insightful because they are nearly impossible to get wrong, often covering both sides of a trait.",
      "Look closely at a typical 'reading' and you find statements that are nearly impossible to get wrong. 'You can be your own worst critic.' 'You have a secret you haven't told many people.' 'Though you seem confident, you sometimes doubt yourself.' Each sounds insightful, but try thinking of one adult it doesn't describe.\n\nSome cover both sides at once, a trick British mentalist Ian Rowland called the 'rainbow ruse': 'You are generally easygoing, but when someone crosses a line, you can be fierce.' Whatever you are like, you hear yourself. Add flattery, such as hidden talents or a big heart, and most people agree. Agreement then feels like proof the reader is gifted."],
    ["Fishing and shotgunning", "Cold readers throw out broad guesses and watch your reactions, so you supply most of the information yourself.",
      "Imagine a medium on stage: 'I'm getting an older man, a father figure, maybe a grandfather or uncle. There's a name with a J, or perhaps an M.' In a room of two hundred, almost everyone has lost an older male relative, and many know a John, James, Mary or Michael. This scattershot approach is called shotgunning. When someone nods, the medium narrows in.\n\nReaders also fish with questions dressed as statements, such as 'Who is the person with the heart problem?' You supply the answer and may later recall the medium telling you. Skilled readers watch faces, posture, clothing, jewellery and accents. It feels like revelation, but you do most of the work."],
    ["Remembered hits, hot reading", "We remember a reader's hits and forget the misses, and some 'psychics' secretly gather real information beforehand.",
      "After a reading, people say the psychic knew things no stranger could. Yet when researchers record sessions, they find plenty of wrong guesses. We remember the astonishing hit and forget the dozen misses. Misses get rescued too: 'Keep that name, it will make sense later,' or the water sensed was a bath, a beach holiday or a leaky tap. This is confirmation bias with flexible meanings.\n\nHot reading means gathering real information beforehand. Today a glance at social media can reveal someone's pets, hometown and children. In the 1980s, sceptic James Randi showed that faith healer Peter Popoff was receiving details about audience members through a hidden earpiece, fed by his wife backstage. When a reader knows something impossible, ask first whether the information was available."],
    ["Scams and testing the claim", "To test a psychic claim, remove every ordinary source of information, and watch for readings used to win trust and then money.",
      "A horoscope or tarot reading can be harmless fun. The danger comes when the same techniques win trust and then money. Some scams begin with a 'psychic' who senses a curse and offers to lift it for a fee, then another fee. Grieving people are especially vulnerable. If you think you have been targeted in Australia, Scamwatch, run by the ACCC, explains how to report it. This is general information, so seek proper advice for your situation.\n\nTo test psychic powers, the reader shouldn't see or hear the person, know anything in advance, or get feedback. Then compare accuracy with chance. Such tests have generally not supported psychic claims, and James Randi's foundation long offered a large prize that was never claimed. Ask: what could they have seen, heard, guessed or looked up?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Forer's students discover about their personality analyses?", ["Each analysis had been written from their own test results","The analyses were drawn at random from a hat","The analyses were the opposite of their real personalities","Every student had received exactly the same text"], 3, "Students rated a generic analysis as highly personal, showing how readily we accept general statements about ourselves."],
    [1, "Why does the 'rainbow ruse' work on almost everyone?", ["It uses private details about each person","It covers both sides of a trait at once","It relies on rare personality traits","It is said too fast for people to think"], 1, "A statement that covers opposite possibilities can't be wrong, so it feels accurate whatever you are like."],
    [2, "Why does shotgunning work in a large audience?", ["Broad guesses fit many, and reactions guide the next","The medium has researched each person in advance","Names beginning with J or M are very rare","People rarely react to what a stranger says"], 0, "In a big room almost every guess fits someone, and their reaction guides the next one."],
    [3, "Why do people leave readings convinced the psychic was accurate?", ["The psychic only makes guesses that are always right","Researchers find psychics are mostly correct","They remember the hits and forget the misses","People rarely discuss readings afterwards"], 2, "Selective memory and flexible meanings turn an ordinary hit rate into an apparent miracle."],
    [4, "What is needed to test a psychic claim fairly?", ["Let the reader chat with the person to build trust","Ask the psychic whether they feel the test was fair","Test only believers who are open to the result","Remove ordinary clues, then compare results with chance"], 3, "If the reader can't see, hear or learn anything about the person, only real ability could beat chance."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A 1948 classroom trick still explains why strangers seem to know you so well."});

WB.L("illusion-of-explanatory-depth", "thinking", "beginner", "Why you understand less than you think",
  "You use a zip every day. Could you explain how it actually works? Most people are surprised by how quickly their confidence collapses.",
  [
    ["Explain a zip", "We feel we understand everyday things deeply until we try to explain how they work.",
      "Try a quick experiment. On a scale of one to seven, how well do you understand how a zip works? Now explain, step by step, how the teeth lock together and how the slider separates them. Most people start confidently and stall within a sentence or two.\n\nIn a well-known 2002 study, Yale psychologists Leonid Rozenblit and Frank Keil asked people to rate their understanding of everyday objects such as zips, flush toilets and locks, then write detailed explanations. After trying, people's ratings dropped noticeably. The researchers named this the illusion of explanatory depth. We feel we understand things deeply because we know what they look like and how to use them."],
    ["The bicycle test", "Knowing how to use something is a different skill from knowing how it works.",
      "Picture a bicycle. Now imagine drawing one accurately: frame, pedals, chain and wheels. Easy, surely. British psychologist Rebecca Lawson tested this in the mid 2000s by asking people to draw or complete diagrams of bicycles. Many made errors that would make a real bike impossible to ride, such as attaching the chain to the front wheel or joining the frame in ways that would stop the handlebars turning. Regular cyclists did better, but even some of them slipped.\n\nThe point isn't that people are foolish. Using something is a different skill from knowing how it works. Our everyday knowledge is mostly useful shortcuts, not a detailed blueprint."],
    ["Why the illusion exists", "Our minds keep rough outlines and lean on the world and other people, so borrowed knowledge feels like our own.",
      "One reason is efficiency. Storing the full workings of every gadget would waste mental effort. You don't need to understand a toilet's valve to flush it. The mind keeps a rough outline and leans on the world as a reference. When you look at a bicycle, the details are right there, so it feels as if they are in your head too. Explanations also come in layers, and each can hide the next. You know a car burns fuel to move, but not how that turns the wheels. We stop at the first layer and mistake it for the bottom.\n\nCognitive scientists Steven Sloman and Philip Fernbach describe knowledge as a community effort. No one understands how to make a pencil from scratch. Because we can look things up or ask someone, community knowledge feels like ours."],
    ["Strong opinions, shallow knowledge", "Asking people to explain how a policy would work, rather than why they support it, exposes gaps and softens extreme views.",
      "Many of us hold firm opinions on complex issues such as tax, health systems or climate policy while knowing little about how the proposals would work. In a 2013 study, Fernbach and colleagues asked people in the United States to rate their understanding of political policies, then explain in detail how those policies would work. Afterwards, people rated their understanding lower and their views tended to become less extreme.\n\nSimply listing reasons for their position didn't have the same effect. Reasons let you stay on familiar ground. Mechanisms force you to confront the gaps. Asking someone to explain the how, rather than defend the why, can make a heated conversation calmer."],
    ["Test and calibrate", "Explaining something out loud or in writing reveals the gaps in your understanding, so hold complex views a little more loosely.",
      "You can turn the illusion into a learning tool. Before you decide you understand something, explain it out loud as if teaching a curious twelve year old. The method is often linked to physicist Richard Feynman. Wherever you stumble or reach for jargon, you have found a gap. Fill it, then try again. Writing works even better, because vague ideas look obviously vague on paper.\n\nNone of this means you should feel embarrassed about not knowing how a zip works. Relying on others is sensible. The lesson is calibration. When you feel very certain about something complex, ask whether you could explain how it works to a sceptical friend. If not, hold your view more loosely. Listen to people who disagree, and be ready to say, 'I'm not sure; let me find out.'"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happened when people tried to explain how everyday objects work?", ["They rated their understanding higher afterwards","They rated their own understanding lower afterwards","Their ratings did not change","They refused to rate the objects"], 1, "Trying to explain exposes the gap between feeling you understand and actually understanding."],
    [1, "What does the bicycle drawing test show?", ["Cyclists never make mistakes when drawing bikes","Drawing skill predicts engineering skill","Using something is not knowing how it works","Most people have never seen a bicycle closely"], 2, "Everyday knowledge is mostly useful shortcuts, not a detailed blueprint."],
    [2, "Why does the illusion of explanatory depth arise?", ["People deliberately pretend to know more than they do","Modern devices are too complex for anyone to use","Memory fails for details we learned as children","The mind keeps rough outlines and leans on others"], 3, "Because details are available in the world or from others, borrowed knowledge feels like our own."],
    [3, "What made people's views less extreme in the 2013 policy study?", ["Explaining in detail how a policy would work","Listing reasons for supporting their position","Reading opposing arguments from other voters","Being asked to rate their confidence higher"], 0, "Explaining mechanisms forces you to confront the gaps, whereas listing reasons keeps you on familiar ground."],
    [4, "What is a good way to find the gaps in your understanding?", ["Re-read the material until it feels familiar","Compare how confident you feel with a friend","Explain it aloud as if teaching a curious child","Look up the topic and decide you understand it"], 2, "Where you stumble or reach for jargon is where your real understanding ends."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Could you explain a zip? Most people's confidence disappears within a sentence."});

WB.L("science-vs-pseudoscience", "thinking", "intermediate", "Science or pseudoscience? How to tell the difference",
  "Astrology, crystal healing and a revolutionary new diet can all sound scientific. A few sharp questions reveal which ideas are willing to be proven wrong.",
  [
    ["Popper and falsifiability", "A claim is scientific if some observation could show it to be false, so a theory that fits every possible result tells you nothing.",
      "In Vienna after the First World War, young Karl Popper was surrounded by bold ideas. Einstein's relativity made a risky prediction: starlight passing the Sun should bend by a specific amount. In 1919, expeditions led by Arthur Eddington measured starlight during a solar eclipse and supported Einstein. The theory could have failed, and it didn't. Some popular theories of psychology and history, by contrast, explained every possible outcome. Popper saw that as a weakness.\n\nHis answer to the problem of separating science from non-science was falsifiability. 'All swans are white' is falsifiable: one black swan breaks it, and Europeans found black swans in Western Australia. 'Invisible fairies tend my garden but leave no trace' isn't. Falsifiable doesn't mean false, just testable. Ask what would prove an idea wrong."],
    ["The warning signs", "Pseudoscience looks scientific but is vague, relies on anecdotes, makes excuses when tests fail and rarely changes.",
      "Pseudoscience is dressed up to look scientific without behaving like science. It shows familiar warning signs. Claims are vague, so they can't easily be checked. Evidence comes mainly from testimonials and anecdotes rather than controlled studies. Scientific words such as 'quantum', 'energy' and 'frequency' are borrowed for glamour without precise meaning. When a test fails, excuses appear: the sceptic's negative energy interfered. The idea hasn't changed for decades despite new evidence. Critics are dismissed as closed-minded. And there is often something to buy.\n\nNo single sign proves an idea is bogus, but when several appear together, look much more carefully before you believe or spend."],
    ["Astrology under test", "When astrologers were tested under blind conditions, they did no better than guessing.",
      "Astrology makes claims that can, in principle, be tested: your birth chart is said to reveal your personality. In a 1985 study in the journal Nature, physicist Shawn Carlson ran a double-blind test with astrologers who had agreed to the method in advance. They were given birth charts and asked to match each to the correct personality profile from a small set of options. Their success rate was about what guessing would produce.\n\nOther studies have looked for links between star signs and personality or career and generally found nothing meaningful. Astrology can still be enjoyable, a prompt for reflection or a bit of fun. But as a scientific claim about how people work, it hasn't passed the test."],
    ["Homeopathy in Australia", "Australia's 2015 NHMRC review found no reliable evidence that homeopathy works for any health condition.",
      "Homeopathy was developed in Germany around 1800 by Samuel Hahnemann. Its central idea is that a substance causing symptoms can cure similar symptoms when heavily diluted, and that more dilution makes it stronger. Many remedies are diluted so far that, statistically, not one molecule of the original substance is likely to remain.\n\nIn 2015, Australia's National Health and Medical Research Council published a major review of the evidence. It concluded there were no health conditions for which there was reliable evidence that homeopathy was effective. The council warned that choosing it over proven treatments may put health at risk.\n\nThis is general information rather than medical advice. Talk to a doctor, and healthdirect is a reliable starting point for health questions in Australia."],
    ["Self-correction, not Galileo", "Science earns trust by testing and correcting itself, and being mocked by experts proves nothing: evidence is what vindicates an idea.",
      "Science gets things wrong all the time. What makes it different is that it has ways of finding and fixing errors. Studies are checked by experts, results are repeated by independent teams, and failed ideas are revised or dropped. For decades, doctors thought stomach ulcers were mainly caused by stress. Perth researchers Barry Marshall and Robin Warren showed that a bacterium, Helicobacter pylori, played a central role. Marshall even swallowed a culture to prove the point. Doctors were sceptical, but evidence won, and the pair received the 2005 Nobel Prize.\n\nFringe promoters often say 'they laughed at Galileo'. But for every rejected idea later proven right, vast numbers were rejected because they were wrong. Galileo was vindicated by his observations, not by being mocked. Being an outsider is fine. Avoiding tests isn't."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes a claim scientific, according to falsifiability?", ["It has never been disproved","A test could show it to be false","Many experts say it is true","It uses careful technical terms"], 1, "A testable claim sticks its neck out, while one that fits every result tells you nothing."],
    [1, "Which is a common warning sign of pseudoscience?", ["Excuses appear when a test fails","Claims are checked by independent teams","Ideas change as new evidence arrives","Results are shared openly for review"], 0, "Blaming failed tests on negative energy or poor conditions protects an idea from ever being proven wrong."],
    [2, "What did the 1985 test of astrologers find?", ["They matched most charts correctly","They did better with longer profiles","Their matches were no better than chance","Some star signs were matched accurately"], 2, "Under double-blind conditions agreed in advance, astrologers could not do better than chance."],
    [3, "What did Australia's 2015 review conclude about homeopathy?", ["It worked for minor conditions only","It matched standard treatment for some illnesses","It worked best at higher dilutions","No condition had reliable evidence it works"], 3, "The review found no reliable evidence of effectiveness and warned against replacing proven treatments."],
    [4, "Why doesn't 'they laughed at Galileo' show an idea is right?", ["Galileo was never actually opposed","Many rejected ideas really were wrong","Experts are never wrong about new ideas","Ideas rejected by experts are always false"], 1, "Marshall and Warren, like Galileo, won on evidence rather than on being doubted."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some ideas are built so they can never be wrong. Here's the test that exposes them."});

WB.L("patterns-in-randomness", "thinking", "intermediate", "Seeing patterns in random noise",
  "Shuffle a playlist and the same artist plays three times in a row. Rigged? Probably not. Randomness is far lumpier than our brains expect.",
  [
    ["Random looks clumpy", "Real randomness contains streaks and clusters that feel deliberate.",
      "Ask one group to invent 100 coin tosses and another to actually toss a coin 100 times. Teachers who run this trick can usually spot the fakes. Invented sequences switch between heads and tails too often and avoid long runs. Real tosses often include a streak of five or six of the same result, because over 100 tosses such runs are quite likely.\n\nOur intuition says random things should look evenly spread, like sprinkles on a cake. In reality, randomness is clumpy. Rain falls unevenly, stars cluster and buses arrive in bunches. When we see a clump, our brains shout 'pattern', even when chance alone would produce it."],
    ["Bombs over London", "Londoners saw a targeting pattern in V-1 strikes, but statistics showed it was chance.",
      "During the Second World War, German V-1 flying bombs fell on London. Many residents were convinced the strikes were targeted, because some neighbourhoods were hit repeatedly while others were spared. Some even moved to apparently safer districts.\n\nIn 1946, a British actuary named R. D. Clarke divided a section of south London into hundreds of equal squares and counted the hits in each. The counts closely matched what you would expect if the bombs had landed at random. The clusters that felt so meaningful were just the natural clumping of chance. This is now called the clustering illusion, and it still shapes how people read crime maps, disease outbreaks and accidents."],
    ["Painting the target", "Drawing the target after the shots makes any pattern look impressive.",
      "Imagine a cowboy firing randomly at a barn. Afterwards, he paints a bullseye around the tightest group of holes and declares himself a sharpshooter. This joke names the Texas sharpshooter fallacy: finding a cluster first, then treating it as if it had been predicted.\n\nIt happens whenever people search lots of data, spot an interesting clump and forget how many other places they looked. A town might seem to have an unusual number of a particular illness. But there are thousands of towns and many kinds of illness, so somewhere, by chance, a striking cluster will appear. Investigators take such reports seriously, and some turn out to be real. Many, though, prove to be painted bullseyes."],
    ["Faces and shuffles", "Our brains expect pattern, so we see faces in noise and distrust true randomness.",
      "In 1976, the Viking 1 spacecraft photographed a hill on Mars that looked startlingly like a human face. Later, sharper images showed an ordinary eroded mesa. Seeing meaningful images in random shapes, especially faces, is called pareidolia. It probably reflects how vital it has been to recognise friends, enemies and emotions quickly.\n\nThe reverse also happens. Users have complained that music shuffles weren't random because songs by one artist played close together. Many apps now adjust shuffle so an artist doesn't repeat, because genuine randomness feels suspicious. Likewise, many people feel that 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 could never win a lottery, though it is exactly as likely as any other combination."],
    ["Test before you trust", "Decide what you're looking for first, check it in fresh data, and stay humble both ways.",
      "Ask how many chances there were for a pattern to appear. A one in a million coincidence is likely in a country of millions. Decide what you are testing before you look, as scientists do by registering their plans in advance. Then check whether the pattern holds in fresh data. A real effect should reappear; a random clump usually won't.\n\nBe humble in both directions. For decades, researchers called basketball's 'hot hand' an illusion. In 2018, Joshua Miller and Adam Sanjurjo found a statistical bias in the original analysis and argued a modest effect may exist after all. 'It's just randomness' can be a mistake too."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How do invented 'random' coin sequences usually differ from real ones?", ["They contain far more long streaks","They switch between heads and tails too often","They contain only heads or only tails","They are identical to real sequences"], 1, "Real randomness is clumpier than we expect, so people invent sequences that look too evenly mixed."],
    [1, "What did the analysis of V-1 strikes on London show?", ["The hits closely matched a random pattern","The bombs targeted wealthy suburbs","Most bombs missed London entirely","The bombs followed the line of the Thames"], 0, "Clusters that felt deliberate were just the natural clumping of chance."],
    [2, "What is the Texas sharpshooter fallacy?", ["Shooting at targets that are too far away","Believing every marksman is accurate","Drawing the target around a cluster after the fact","Ignoring evidence from small towns"], 2, "A cluster found after searching the data proves little, because you didn't predict it first."],
    [3, "Why do music apps often adjust shuffle so an artist doesn't repeat?", ["Truly random lists are too hard to code","Repeated songs slow the streaming service","Storage limits stop an artist repeating","True randomness feels rigged to listeners"], 3, "We expect random to look evenly mixed, so real randomness often feels suspicious."],
    [4, "What is a sound way to test a suspicious pattern?", ["Search until you find a striking cluster","Check whether it holds up in fresh data","Trust it if it feels convincing","Dismiss every pattern as chance"], 1, "A real effect should show up again; a random clump usually won't."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Shuffle your playlist and the same artist plays three times running. Rigged?"});

WB.L("regression-to-the-mean", "thinking", "advanced", "Regression to the mean: why extremes don't last",
  "A footballer has a dream season, then a so-so one. A new treatment seems to cure your worst week. Often the real cause is a quiet statistical force.",
  [
    ["The flight instructors", "Praise seemed to make pilots worse and criticism seemed to make them better.",
      "The psychologist Daniel Kahneman told of teaching Israeli air force flight instructors. One objected that praise works better than punishment. When he praised an excellent manoeuvre, he said, the next attempt was usually worse. When he shouted at a bad one, the next was usually better.\n\nKahneman realised the instructor saw something real but drew the wrong conclusion. An unusually good flight is partly skill and partly luck, and luck tends not to repeat. So the next attempt is likely to be closer to average, whatever the instructor says. The same goes for unusually bad flights. The praise and shouting were mostly irrelevant. This pattern is called regression to the mean."],
    ["Skill plus luck", "Any result that mixes skill and luck drifts back towards average after an extreme.",
      "Think of any performance as skill plus luck. Skill is fairly stable. Luck bounces around randomly. When you pick out an extreme result, the very best or worst, you are partly selecting for skill and partly for unusually good or bad luck. Next time the skill remains, but the luck is likely to be more ordinary, so the result moves back towards typical.\n\nCrucially, no cause is needed. Nobody got lazy or complacent. The more luck matters, the stronger the pull to average. Pure skill wouldn't regress; pure luck would regress completely.\n\nFrancis Galton noticed it in the 1880s: very tall parents tended to have tall children, but on average not quite as tall. It describes groups, not destiny."],
    ["Curses and jinxes", "Many famous 'curses' are just regression in disguise.",
      "People joke about the Sports Illustrated cover jinx: athletes on the American magazine's cover often seem to slump soon after. Similar talk follows big award winners and players who sign large contracts.\n\nBut think about when someone lands on a cover. It is usually after a remarkable stretch, often their best ever, which almost certainly involved some good luck. The following season is likely to be closer to their usual level, which looks like a slump by comparison. No curse required.\n\nThe same logic applies to businesses praised after a record year, fund managers who topped the charts and restaurants that have just won an award. Picking people at their peak practically guarantees an apparent decline afterwards."],
    ["Medicine and control groups", "People often seek treatment at their worst and improve anyway, so trials need control groups.",
      "When are you most likely to try a new remedy for back pain, a headache or a cold? Usually when symptoms are at their worst. Many conditions naturally fluctuate, so after a bad patch things are likely to improve somewhat, whatever you do. If you took a remedy at the peak, it will seem to have worked.\n\nThat is a major reason researchers use control groups. In a well designed trial, some patients get the treatment and others get a placebo or standard care, and both groups are chosen in the same way. Regression affects both equally, so only a genuine difference between them shows a real effect. This is general information, so speak to a health professional about your own situation."],
    ["Blackspots and big claims", "Expect extremes to look less extreme next time, and ask what would have happened anyway.",
      "Imagine cameras installed at the intersections with the most crashes last year. Next year, crashes there fall. Success? Partly, perhaps. But some sites topped the list through an unusually unlucky year and would have improved anyway. Road safety researchers account for this by comparing treated sites with similar untreated ones, or by using several years of data. The trap also appears in schools, where extra help goes to the lowest scorers, and in workplaces, where struggling teams get a new manager.\n\nSo when something is chosen for being extreme, expect it to look less extreme next time, and ask what would have happened without the intervention. Kahneman's instructors weren't wrong about what they saw, only about why."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the flight instructor's criticism seem to work?", ["Criticism motivates people more than praise does","Trainees tried harder out of fear of him","Poor flights were usually followed by average ones","Shouting helped him give clearer instructions"], 2, "Extreme results tend to be followed by more ordinary ones, whatever anyone says in between."],
    [1, "Which kind of activity shows the strongest regression to the mean?", ["One where luck plays a big role","One based purely on skill","One with no measured results","One done only once"], 0, "The more luck matters, the more an extreme result is likely to fall back towards average."],
    [2, "Why might an athlete slump after appearing on a magazine cover?", ["The cover really does bring a curse","Fame always destroys an athlete's focus","The cover came after an unusually lucky peak","Athletes stop training after the photo shoot"], 2, "Picking people at their peak practically guarantees an apparent decline afterwards."],
    [3, "Why do medical trials use control groups?", ["To make each trial cheaper to run","Because placebos cure most illnesses","To check the doctors rather than the drug","To show what improvement happens anyway"], 3, "People often treat themselves at their worst and improve anyway, so a fair comparison is essential."],
    [4, "Why might crashes fall at camera sites even without cameras?", ["Drivers stop using those intersections afterwards","Sites were chosen after unusually bad years","Crash figures are never recorded accurately","The cameras were fitted at random sites"], 1, "Selecting the worst spots builds in some improvement through regression alone."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Praise a great performance and the next is worse. Scold a bad one and it improves."});

WB.L("berksons-paradox", "thinking", "advanced", "Berkson's paradox: when filtering creates false patterns",
  "Why do the best restaurants seem to be in the worst locations, and why do attractive dates seem more likely to be rude? The answer is a sneaky trick of selection.",
  [
    ["A strange hospital pattern", "Looking only at hospital patients can create links between diseases that don't exist outside.",
      "In 1946, the American statistician Joseph Berkson pointed out a puzzle in medical research. Among hospital patients, two unrelated conditions could appear linked, found together more or less often than in the general population.\n\nThe reason was how patients got into the data. People are admitted because they have one condition or another, or both. Studying only hospital patients filters the population by those very conditions, and that filtering alone can create a statistical relationship that doesn't exist in the wider world. This is now called Berkson's paradox. It is a cousin of selection bias, and once you know it, you spot it in dating, hiring, restaurants and headlines."],
    ["The dating puzzle", "If you only date people who are kind or attractive, those traits will seem to clash.",
      "The mathematician Jordan Ellenberg popularised this example. Suppose someone will only date a person who is reasonably kind, reasonably attractive, or both. In the general population, imagine kindness and looks are unrelated.\n\nNow look only at the people this person actually dates. Anyone neither kind nor attractive has been filtered out. Among those left, the attractive ones only needed to clear one bar, so many aren't especially kind. The kind ones are often not especially attractive. Within this group, looks and kindness seem to be opposites, and it feels as though attractive people are more likely to be rude. Nothing about the people changed. The filter created the pattern."],
    ["Draw it to see it", "Selecting on a combination of two traits manufactures a relationship between them.",
      "Picture a square scatter plot with talent along one axis and charm along the other. If the traits are unrelated, the dots fill the square evenly, with no tilt.\n\nNow add a rule: only people whose talent plus charm exceeds a certain total are admitted. Draw a diagonal line across the lower left corner and remove every dot beneath it. The remaining dots now slope downwards. Among those who passed, the most talented tend to be less charming, and the most charming less talented. A negative correlation has appeared from nowhere.\n\nThat is the essence of the paradox. It also explains why great food can seem to hide in awkward locations: a restaurant in a bad spot survives only if the food is excellent."],
    ["Colliders and selection", "Filtering or controlling for something influenced by two factors can create a false link between them.",
      "Researchers call a variable influenced by two other factors a 'collider'. Filtering on it, or statistically controlling for it, can create a misleading link between those factors.\n\nA well-known puzzle involves birth weight. Babies of smokers tend to be lighter, and low birth weight raises risk. Yet among only low birth weight babies, some studies found those born to smokers did slightly better. Smoking wasn't protective. Low birth weight can come from smoking or from other, often more serious problems, so comparing within that group mixed very different situations.\n\nThe same trap appears in admissions. Among admitted students, a weak school record must be offset by a strong entrance test, so the test may seem to predict nothing. Hiring shows it too, and in early COVID-19 studies of only tested or hospitalised patients."],
    ["Spotting the filter", "Always ask how the cases in a dataset got there.",
      "The key defence is a simple question: how did these cases end up in front of me? If the group was selected on the very traits you're comparing, be suspicious of any pattern within it.\n\nYour social circle, your newsfeed, a hospital ward, a list of successful companies and the people you have dated are all filtered samples. Patterns within them may be produced by the filter rather than the world. When possible, look at a broader, unfiltered group. When that isn't possible, be careful about drawing big conclusions.\n\nData never simply speaks for itself. It always arrives through some process, and understanding that process is half the work of understanding the data."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did hospital data suggest links between unrelated conditions?", ["Doctors recorded the data incorrectly","Hospitals themselves cause disease","Admission filtered patients by those conditions","The diseases were highly contagious"], 2, "Studying only admitted patients filters the population by the very conditions being compared."],
    [1, "In the dating example, why do kindness and attractiveness seem opposed?", ["Those lacking both traits were filtered out","Attractive people really are less kind","Kindness makes people look less attractive","Dating apps hide kinder people from view"], 0, "Within a filtered group, each trait can excuse a lack of the other, which creates a false trade-off."],
    [2, "What happens when you remove the lower left corner of an unrelated scatter plot?", ["The dots spread out more evenly","The remaining dots slope downwards","The dots form a neat circle","The correlation becomes perfectly positive"], 1, "Selecting on a combined total manufactures a negative relationship between the two traits."],
    [3, "What is a 'collider' in research?", ["A type of particle accelerator","A large randomised trial","A variable that causes everything else","A variable influenced by two other factors"], 3, "Filtering or controlling for a collider can create a false link between its two causes."],
    [4, "What is the best first question to ask of a surprising pattern in a dataset?", ["How big is the dataset?","Who paid for the study?","How did these cases get selected?","Is the result significant?"], 2, "If the sample was selected on the traits you're comparing, the filter may have created the pattern."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why do the best restaurants seem to hide in the worst spots? Blame the filter."});

